<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xiangyang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xiangyang is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which with its twin city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fancheng">Fancheng</a> formed the gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> valley. The Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-xiangyang">siege of Xiangyang</a> from 1267 to 1273 was the pivotal confrontation in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>. The city fell only after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counterweight-trebuchet">counterweight trebuchets</a> built by Persian engineers were deployed, opening the way for the Yuan conquest of southern China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xiangyang.jpg" alt="Xiangyang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zihanzor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiangyang,_Hubei,_China.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
